from _lib import *

write("style-guides",
 "Lightweight style guides show a button's colour, padding and maybe a hover state, then leave developers to invent the rest. A button has a dozen or more states, and each one is a UX decision someone will make — designed or not.",
 [("A button has more than three states",
   "Typical Figma guide: default, hover, disabled, and “dev will figure out the rest”.\n\nWhat a button actually needs:\n\n• **focus-visible** and **pressed (active)**\n• **selected**, plus selected + hover and selected + focus\n• **loading** and indeterminate\n• **error, warning, success, confirmation**",
   cmp("Typical style guide", ["Default", "Hover", "Disabled"],
       "What a button needs", ["Focus-visible, pressed", "Selected, and its combinations", "Loading, error, success"]),
   q("Which state does Calabro see skipped most often?",
     ["Pressed", "Hover", "Disabled", "Focus-visible for keyboard users"],
     "It is the user's immediate proof that something happened.")),
  ("Why combined states exist",
   "People switch between mouse and keyboard mid-task. That produces states like **selected + focused** or **selected + hovered** that nobody specified.\n\n**focus-visible** must be distinct from hover — keyboard and assistive-tech users rely on it.\n\n**Loading** belongs on anything tied to a background process, with a **confirmation** (“Submitted”) afterwards.", None,
   q("Why must the focus style differ from the hover style?",
     ["Keyboard users rely on it to know where they are", "Hover styles don't render on touch devices", "Focus styles have stricter contrast requirements", "Design systems usually define them separately"],
     "Reusing the hover style leaves keyboard users without a position indicator.")),
  ("Who makes the decision if you don't",
   "Visual values belong in tokens and CSS. **The guide's job is states and behaviour.**\n\nWhen the guide says “you figure it out”, developers make interaction decisions without UX context — and the inconsistency shows up later as bugs, accessibility failures and components that behave differently in each part of the product.", None,
   q("What belongs in the style guide rather than in tokens?",
     ["States and behaviour", "Hex values and spacing scales", "Typography and font weights", "Border radius and shadow depth"],
     "Visual values live in tokens; the guide documents what the component does."))],
 [q("What is the real cost of an incomplete style guide?",
    ["Developers make UX decisions without context", "Designers spend longer in review", "Components take longer to build", "The design system falls out of date"],
    "The inconsistency surfaces as bugs and accessibility failures."),
  q("What does the pressed state give the user?",
    ["Immediate proof the action registered", "A preview of the resulting page", "An indication the control is enabled", "A visual match with the hover state"],
    "It is instant system feedback."),
  q("When should a button have a loading state?",
    ["Whenever it triggers a background process", "Only on forms that submit to a server", "Only when the wait exceeds five seconds", "Whenever the design system provides one"],
    "With a confirmation afterwards."),
  q("Why do combined states matter?",
    ["Users switch between mouse and keyboard", "Designers forget to specify them", "They are required by WCAG 2.2", "They appear only in dark mode"],
    "Selected + focused, selected + hovered and similar combinations occur in real use."),
  q("Which is a behaviour question rather than a visual one?",
    ["What happens while the action is processing", "Which grey the disabled state uses", "How much padding the label gets", "Which corner radius the component has"],
    "Behaviour is what the guide must specify.")],
 summary="A button has a dozen states. Document behaviour, or a developer will decide it for you.")

write("conversational-ux",
 "Conversational UX isn't a chatty tone. Moore and Arar's Natural Conversation Framework is about interaction mechanics: turn-taking, context, repair and shared meaning.",
 [("Four mechanics of a real conversation",
   "**Turn-taking** — respond to what the user just did.\n**Context** — fit where they are in the workflow.\n**Repair** — help them recover when something breaks.\n**Shared meaning** — use the user's words, not the system's internal names.\n\nFramed this way, progressive disclosure is a conversational move: you don't tell a new acquaintance your whole life story.",
   cmp("Monologue dressed as conversation", ["Auto-starting tour", "“Friendly” upsell modal", "“Something went wrong”"],
       "Actual conversation", ["Responds to the user's last action", "Fits the workflow stage", "Helps repair the problem"]),
   q("Which mechanic does “Something went wrong” fail hardest?",
     ["Repair", "Turn-taking", "Context", "Shared meaning"],
     "It gives the user nothing to recover with.")),
  ("The B2B example",
   "A user logs into an analytics tool to export last month's report.\n\nAn unrequested tour introduces Workspaces, Automations and Integrations. A modal pushes an upgrade. The export fails with “Something went wrong”. The settings page leads with add-on marketing.\n\nNone of it starts from user intent, respects task order, supports repair or builds shared meaning — however friendly the wording is.", None,
   q("What is wrong with an auto-starting tour, in conversational terms?",
     ["It ignores what the user came to do", "It uses too much text at once", "It can't be dismissed on mobile", "It repeats information from the signup flow"],
     "No turn-taking: it starts talking before the user has done anything.")),
  ("The paper test",
   "**1.** Print the words from an onboarding tour, modal, paywall, error or settings page.\n**2.** One colleague plays “the system” and may only say what's printed.\n**3.** Another plays the user, with a real goal.\n\nIf the system can only interrupt, repeat itself, or fail to answer, you have a monologue — and casual wording can disguise interruptions, shaming or upsells.", None,
   q("In the paper test, what may the person playing the system say?",
     ["Only the words that are printed", "Anything a support agent would say", "Whatever the design intent was", "Only answers to direct questions"],
     "The constraint is the point: it exposes a monologue immediately."))],
 [q("What is conversational UX actually about?",
    ["How an interaction unfolds", "How friendly the wording is", "Whether the product has a chatbot", "How closely copy matches the brand voice"],
    "Turn-taking, context, repair and shared meaning."),
  q("Whose framework does Calabro use?",
    ["Moore and Arar's", "Nielsen and Molich's", "Pirolli and Card's", "Kurosu and Kashimura's"],
    "The Natural Conversation Framework, 2017."),
  q("What does “shared meaning” require?",
    ["Using the user's words, not system names", "Defining key terms in a glossary", "Matching the reading level to the audience", "Keeping terminology consistent across screens"],
    "Internal feature names break it."),
  q("Why is progressive disclosure a conversational move?",
    ["You don't tell a new acquaintance everything at once", "It reduces the number of words on screen", "It lets users set their own pace", "It mirrors how chatbots stream responses"],
    "Information arrives when it is relevant to the exchange."),
  q("What can casual wording disguise?",
    ["Interruptions, shaming and upsells", "Slow system performance", "Missing accessibility features", "Inconsistent visual design"],
    "Which is why the test is whether the language helps the task.")],
 summary="Turn-taking, context, repair, shared meaning — not a friendly tone of voice.")

write("checkout",
 "Most cart abandonment comes from surprises and friction, not indecision. Baymard's survey of US shoppers puts unexpected costs at the top by a wide margin.",
 [("Why shoppers abandon",
   "From Baymard's survey as cited in 2024:\n\n• Extra costs too high — **47%**\n• Forced account creation — **25%**\n• Delivery too slow — **24%**\n• Didn't trust the site — **19%**\n• Checkout too long — **18%**\n• Couldn't see the total up front — **17%**\n• Returns policy — **16%**\n• Site errors or crashes — **14%**\n\nBaymard updates these figures regularly — check the current release before quoting them.", None,
   q("What is the single biggest reason shoppers abandon checkout?",
     ["Extra costs appearing late", "Being forced to create an account", "Slow delivery options", "A checkout flow with too many steps"],
     "47% in the cited survey — nearly twice the next reason.")),
  ("Price, account, delivery",
   "**Show all costs up front** and every change as it happens; keep a running total visible.\n\n**Make guest checkout prominent** and offer an account after the purchase, not before.\n\n**State delivery times before checkout**, not on the final screen.",
   cmp("Surprises that lose the sale", ["Shipping added at the last step", "Account required to continue", "Delivery date revealed after payment"],
       "What to do", ["Running total, visible throughout", "Guest checkout, account offered later", "Delivery times before checkout"]),
   q("When should you offer account creation?",
     ["After the purchase", "Before the cart page", "At the payment step", "Only to returning customers"],
     "Forced account creation is the second-largest abandonment reason.")),
  ("Trust, length and recovery",
   "Earn trust at the payment step with recognisable security cues and clear assurances about data.\n\nCut checkout to essentials and autofill everything you can. Offer the wallets people already use — Apple Pay, Google Pay, PayPal.\n\nMake the return policy easy to find and fair. And handle errors without wiping what the user typed.", None,
   q("A card is declined. What should the form do?",
     ["Keep everything the user entered", "Return the user to the cart", "Clear the card fields for security", "Offer a different payment method only"],
     "Site errors are an abandonment cause in their own right; preserving input is basic recovery."))],
 [q("Where do these percentages come from?",
    ["A Baymard survey of US shoppers, as cited in 2024", "Calabro's own e-commerce client data", "A Nielsen Norman Group benchmark study", "Aggregated analytics from retail platforms"],
    "And the article notes they should be re-checked before quoting."),
  q("What is the main cause of abandonment, in one word?",
    ["Surprises", "Indecision", "Price", "Speed"],
    "Unexpected costs, hidden totals and late delivery news."),
  q("What should stay visible throughout checkout?",
    ["A running total", "The returns policy", "A progress bar", "A support chat widget"],
    "Including every change as it happens."),
  q("Why offer familiar wallets?",
    ["Users already trust and can use them", "They reduce payment processing fees", "They remove the need for a privacy policy", "They are required in most European markets"],
    "Recognition reduces both friction and distrust at the payment step."),
  q("Which two reasons sit at 19% and 18% in the cited data?",
    ["Didn't trust the site; checkout too long", "Returns policy; site errors", "Delivery too slow; hidden total", "Forced account; extra costs"],
    "Trust and length sit in the middle of the list.")],
 summary="Unexpected costs (47%) lead the list. Show the total early, allow guest checkout, keep the input on error.")

write("c-confirm-undo",
 "Choose by frequency and severity. Frequent, low-impact actions get Undo; rare, severe ones get a confirmation — typed, if the stakes are high.",
 [("The severity × frequency grid",
   "**Frequent + low impact** → Undo by default.\n**Rare + low impact** → one click, no interruption.\n**Frequent + severe** → Undo, plus a warning.\n**Rare + severe** → confirmation, typed if the stakes are high.\n\nMost actions are reversible, so Undo is the default. Confirmation interrupts the flow — make sure it's worth it.",
   cmp("Confirmation", ["Rare and severe", "Typed confirm for the worst cases", "Interrupts deliberately"],
       "Undo", ["Frequent and low impact", "Persistent button, not a toast", "No interruption"]),
   q("Users archive items dozens of times a day. Which guardrail fits?",
     ["Undo", "A confirmation dialog", "A typed confirmation", "A warning banner above the list"],
     "Frequent and low impact: don't interrupt, let them reverse it.")),
  ("Truly irreversible actions",
   "Only a few actions genuinely can't be undone:\n\n• Deleting linked items\n• Removing data from other people's accounts\n• Disconnecting devices\n• Leaving without saving\n\n**If you can't make it undoable, delay it** — for example, 15 minutes before a permanent delete actually runs.", None,
   q("A delete can't be reversed by the system. What's the fallback?",
     ["Delay the action so it can still be cancelled", "Show two confirmation dialogs in sequence", "Require an admin to approve it", "Email the user a copy of the deleted data"],
     "A delay turns an irreversible action into a reversible window.")),
  ("Label the outcome, not the dialog",
   "Use **specific verbs**: Delete, Leave, Unsubscribe, Stay. Add “forever” or “permanently” when it is.\n\nNo **double negatives** — “Cancel cancellation?” is a trap.\n\nFor high-severity actions, ask the user to **type a name** to break the instinctive click.\n\nAnd put Undo in a **persistent button**, not only a toast that fades away.", None,
   q("What is wrong with “Yes” and “Cancel” as dialog buttons?",
     ["They don't say what will happen", "They are too short to tap accurately", "They don't translate consistently", "They imply the action is reversible"],
     "Label the outcome: Delete forever, Stay on this page."))],
 [q("What is the default guardrail, according to this note?",
    ["Undo", "A confirmation dialog", "A warning message", "A permissions check"],
    "Most actions are reversible, so Undo should be the norm."),
  q("When is a typed confirmation worth it?",
    ["Rare actions with high stakes", "Any deletion of user data", "Actions performed by administrators", "Any action affecting more than one record"],
    "Typing breaks the instinctive click."),
  q("Why is a toast a poor place for Undo?",
    ["It disappears before users react", "It can't be styled consistently", "Screen readers announce it twice", "It blocks part of the interface"],
    "Undo should be a persistent button."),
  q("Which action is genuinely irreversible?",
    ["Removing data from another person's account", "Archiving a project", "Changing a notification setting", "Deleting a draft you just wrote"],
    "Along with disconnecting devices and leaving without saving."),
  q("What does “Cancel cancellation?” illustrate?",
    ["A double negative in a dialog", "An action that needs typed confirmation", "A missing undo path", "An over-used confirmation step"],
    "Specific verbs avoid the trap.")],
 summary="Undo by default; confirm when rare and severe; label the outcome, never “Yes”.")

write("c-selection-controls",
 "Radio for a question with one answer, checkbox for a statement or independent options, toggle only when the change applies immediately. Never pre-select.",
 [("Six controls, six jobs",
   "**Radio** — the label is a question; exactly one answer; options equally weighted; good for required input.\n**Checkbox** — the label is a statement; multi-select or a single confirmation; good for optional input.\n**Toggle** — a binary setting that takes effect immediately.\n**Segmented** — 2–5 short options, single select.\n**Dropdown** — single choice from a long list (5+).\n**Chips** — short multi-select, often filters.",
   cmp("Common mistakes", ["Pre-selected radio or checkbox", "Dropdown for 3 options", "Toggle that needs a Save button"],
       "Rule", ["Nothing pre-filled", "Segmented control for 2–5", "Toggle = applies instantly"]),
   q("A settings change only takes effect after the user clicks Save. Which control is wrong for it?",
     ["A toggle", "A checkbox", "A radio button", "A segmented control"],
     "A toggle promises immediate effect.")),
  ("Never pre-select",
   "In usability testing, **pre-selected options are consistently overlooked and submitted unchanged**.\n\nA pre-filled answer isn't a default the user chose — it's an answer they never gave.\n\nSort options from most to least common, and offer a way out: “None of the above”, “I don't remember”.", None,
   q("What happens to pre-selected options in testing?",
     ["Users overlook them and submit unchanged", "Users change them more often than blank ones", "Users read them more carefully", "Users skip the whole question"],
     "Which is why the recommendation is absolute.")),
  ("Size and layout",
   "Targets around **40×40 px**.\n\nUse a dropdown only when the list is long (5 or more) and single-choice; below that, a segmented control or radio group shows everything at once.\n\nChips suit short multi-select such as filters — not single-choice questions styled to look modern.", None,
   q("You have three mutually exclusive, short options. What should you use?",
     ["A segmented control or radio group", "A dropdown", "Chips", "A toggle with three positions"],
     "Dropdowns hide the options; with three, show them all."))],
 [q("When is the label of a control a question?",
    ["Radio buttons", "Checkboxes", "Toggles", "Chips"],
    "Radio: one question, exactly one answer."),
  q("What is the threshold for a dropdown?",
    ["Five or more single-choice options", "Three or more options", "Ten or more options", "Any list that doesn't fit on one line"],
    "Below that, show the options."),
  q("Which control suits a single optional confirmation, such as agreeing to terms?",
    ["A checkbox", "A radio button", "A toggle", "A segmented control"],
    "The label is a statement the user agrees with or doesn't."),
  q("How should options be ordered?",
    ["Most to least common", "Alphabetically, always", "Shortest to longest label", "In the order the system stores them"],
    "With a way out such as “None of the above”."),
  q("What are chips best for?",
    ["Short multi-select, such as filters", "Single-choice questions", "Binary settings", "Long lists of records"],
    "Styling radio buttons as chips is listed as a mistake.")],
 summary="Radio = one question; checkbox = a statement; toggle = instant. Never pre-select.")

write("c-tables-bulk",
 "Complex tables and bulk import are the B2B work most products get wrong — and the import succeeds or fails in its repair step.",
 [("Tables start from tasks",
   "Work out which filters, sorts, search, editing modes and permissions are actually needed.\n\n• Let users build their own filter set; consider per-column filters for speed\n• Let users show, hide and reorder columns when comparison matters\n• Offer compact and comfortable **density modes**\n• Test with very long and very short values, and with pseudo-localisation\n• Check how a screen reader moves through the table", None,
   q("Why offer density modes?",
     ["Different users need different row counts on screen", "Compact mode improves rendering performance", "It is required for WCAG compliance", "It compensates for long translated strings"],
     "Some users scan many rows; others read a few in detail.")),
  ("Row actions must be visible",
   "**Avoid hover-only row actions.** They cause errors and rage clicks, they don't exist on touch, and keyboard users can't reach them predictably.\n\nUse visible buttons on each row.",
   cmp("Hover-only actions", ["Invisible until you find them", "Nothing on touch devices", "Rage clicks and mis-clicks"],
       "Visible row buttons", ["Discoverable at a glance", "Work on every input", "Predictable for keyboard users"]),
   q("What does the note say about hover-reveal row actions?",
     ["They cause errors and rage clicks", "They work if the hover area is large", "They are acceptable on desktop-only tools", "They should be paired with a tooltip"],
     "Use visible buttons instead.")),
  ("Five stages of bulk import",
   "**1. Pre-import** — template, example file, guardrails\n**2. Upload** — drag, keyboard, copy-paste\n**3. Map** — match columns, check values\n**4. Repair** — show only the error rows; dedupe\n**5. Import** — summary, and tag the new records\n\nMost products dismiss a file as corrupt or invalid. **The value is in helping users fix it in place.**", None,
   q("Which stage decides whether a bulk import succeeds?",
     ["Repair", "Upload", "Mapping", "Pre-import"],
     "Letting users fix mismatches inside the flow instead of rejecting the file."))],
 [q("What is the most common bulk-import failure?",
    ["Rejecting the file instead of helping fix it", "Losing data during the upload", "Importing duplicate records silently", "Timing out on large files"],
    "“Invalid file” ends the task; a repair step completes it."),
  q("What should the repair step show?",
    ["Only the rows with errors", "The full file with errors highlighted", "A downloadable error report", "The first ten problems found"],
    "Plus deduplication."),
  q("When should users be able to reorder columns?",
    ["When comparison matters", "Whenever the table has more than five columns", "Only in admin-level views", "When the table is printed or exported"],
    "Along with showing and hiding them."),
  q("What should you test a table with?",
    ["Very long and very short values", "At least a thousand rows", "Three different screen sizes", "Every supported browser"],
    "Plus pseudo-localisation, to catch layouts that break in translation."),
  q("What happens at the final import stage?",
    ["A summary, and the new records get tagged", "The file is archived for audit", "Users are emailed a confirmation", "The table refreshes automatically"],
    "Tagging lets users find what they just imported.")],
 summary="Visible row actions, user-built filters, and a repair step that fixes the import instead of rejecting it.")
